  "output": "I have all the evidence needed. Here is the analysis.\n\n## Code evidence\n\n**`pipeline/promote.py:1-13`** (entire file):\n\n```python\ndef promote(req):\n    if req.failed:\n        rollback(req)      # line 3\n    else:\n        cleanup(req)       # line 5\n\n\ndef rollback(req):\n    return req             # line 9\n\n\ndef cleanup(req):\n    return req             # line 13\n```\n\n### Why both cannot run in one `promote()` call\n\n1. **Mutually exclusive branches.** `rollback(req)` (line 3) is the body of the `if` suite; `cleanup(req)` (line 5) is the body of the `else` suite. Python's `if/else` executes exactly one suite per evaluation; the condition `req.failed` is read once at line 2. Even if `rollback(req)` mutated `req.failed`, the `else` suite was already skipped — there is no re-test, no loop, no fall-through.\n\n2. **No exception path can cross branches.** There is no `try`/`except`/`finally`, no `with`, and no loop anywhere in the function (lines 1-5), so `cleanup` cannot be reached from a failing `rollback` (or vice versa).\n\n3. **The callees are inert and terminate.** `rollback` (lines 8-9) and `cleanup` (lines 12-13) each just `return req`. Neither calls the other, and neither calls `promote`, so there is no recursion/re-entrancy that could re-enter the other branch.\n\n4. **No competing definitions or callers.** A repo-wide grep for `rollback|cleanup|promote` returns matches only in `README.md`, `pipeline/promote.py`, and `pipeline/deploy.py` — and `deploy.py:16` defines its own separate `cleanup(build)`, invoked only from `deploy()` at `deploy.py:5`, never from `promote`. The modules contain **zero import statements** (grep for `import|from ` → no matches), so nothing can reach into `promote.py` to add behavior.\n\n5. **Code-graph corroboration** — `python -m provenlattice explain-symbol pipeline.promote.promote --json` yields:\n   - `E-CALL-e10dc11ffc5fddc998fa9251`: `\"pipeline.promote.promote CALLS pipeline.promote.cleanup\"` (confidence 0.98)\n   - `E-CALL-e4237d4a479ed5c93ee6262b`: `\"pipeline.promote.promote CALLS pipeline.promote.rollback\"` (confidence 0.98)\n\n   i.e., the graph records two independent CALLS edges but carries no branch-exclusivity fact — which the fixture's own `README.md:9` flags as the intended \"hidden fact\": *\"rollback / cleanup sit in mutually exclusive if/else branches\"*.\n\nThe only way both could fire in one invocation would be if `promote` were restructured (e.g., sequential `if`/`if` instead of `if`/`else`), which the source shows it is not.\n\nANSWER: 不可能 — 在 `promote()`（pipeline/promote.py:1-5）中，`rollback(req)` 位于 `if req.failed:` 分支（第3行），`cleanup(req)` 位于 `else` 分支（第5行），二者互斥，任一次调用最多且恰好执行其中一个；无 try/finally、无循环、无重入（两个被调函数仅 `return req`），全仓库无 import 及其他调用点，代码图（explain-symbol）也仅给出两条独立 CALLS 边而不含分支互斥信息。"
